\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Lift and drag directions over the hover wingbeat ($J=0$), for the four sign combinations of the pitch angle $\psi $ and the stroke phase $\sin \tau ^*$, drawn in the stroke frame. The wing velocity $\vec  {v}^*$ is purely in-plane, so drag is purely in-plane, and lift is purely out-of-plane. The hollow circle marks the leading edge. The pitch angle $\psi $, measured from $\hat  {n}$, and the angle of attack $\alpha $ of \autoref {eq:alpha-hover}, measured from $\hat  {v}$, are marked as in \autoref {fig:flow-angles}. $\alpha $ quadrants I--IV are marked as in \autoref {fig:liftdrag-quadrants}. The ``useful'' quadrants (lift along $\hat  {n}$) are I and IV.}}{10}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hover-quadrants}{{6}{10}{Lift and drag directions over the hover wingbeat ($J=0$), for the four sign combinations of the pitch angle $\psi $ and the stroke phase $\sin \tau ^*$, drawn in the stroke frame. The wing velocity $\vec {v}^*$ is purely in-plane, so drag is purely in-plane, and lift is purely out-of-plane. The hollow circle marks the leading edge. The pitch angle $\psi $, measured from $\hat {n}$, and the angle of attack $\alpha $ of \autoref {eq:alpha-hover}, measured from $\hat {v}$, are marked as in \autoref {fig:flow-angles}. $\alpha $ quadrants I--IV are marked as in \autoref {fig:liftdrag-quadrants}. The ``useful'' quadrants (lift along $\hat {n}$) are I and IV}{figure.caption.7}{}}
