\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.2}Hover}{27}{subsection.4.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Hover hold under the velocity law (\autoref {eq:fdes}, $K = 2.7$). The velocity perturbation is eliminated within about one time unit. The position offset accumulated meanwhile (about $0.05$ body lengths) is never corrected---there is no position feedback---and some slow residual drift remains, but the body settles into a clean hover about the settled point.}}{28}{figure.caption.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hover}{{19}{28}{Hover hold under the velocity law (\autoref {eq:fdes}, $K = 2.7$). The velocity perturbation is eliminated within about one time unit. The position offset accumulated meanwhile (about $0.05$ body lengths) is never corrected---there is no position feedback---and some slow residual drift remains, but the body settles into a clean hover about the settled point}{figure.caption.23}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.3}Prescribed Trajectory}{28}{subsection.4.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Gain sensitivity of the hover recovery of \autoref {fig:hover}, swept over $K T^*$ (gain $\times $ wingbeat period). Open circle: the gain $K = 2.7$ used throughout; dotted line: the one-beat gain $K T^* = 1$; shaded band: beyond the predicted stability bound $K T^* = 2$. (a) Per-beat contraction of the velocity error (least-squares fit of $\vec  {e}_{n+1} = r\, \vec  {e}_n$ over the transient beats), against the discrete-model line $1 - K T^*$; in the unstable band the fit reflects bounded wandering, not contraction. (b) Time for the beat-sampled error to settle below $5\%$ of the perturbation; the last stable gain ($K T^* = 2.1$) settles off scale at $16$, and unstable gains never settle. (c) Accumulated position offset, against the discrete prediction $|\vec  {u}^*(0)| \left ( 1/K - T^*/2 \right )$; unstable gains wander off scale. (d) Fraction of transient beats on which the trim saturates: zero across the stable range, nonzero only past the stability boundary, where the saturation bounds the divergence.}}{29}{figure.caption.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gain-sweep}{{20}{29}{Gain sensitivity of the hover recovery of \autoref {fig:hover}, swept over $K T^*$ (gain $\times $ wingbeat period). Open circle: the gain $K = 2.7$ used throughout; dotted line: the one-beat gain $K T^* = 1$; shaded band: beyond the predicted stability bound $K T^* = 2$. (a) Per-beat contraction of the velocity error (least-squares fit of $\vec {e}_{n+1} = r\, \vec {e}_n$ over the transient beats), against the discrete-model line $1 - K T^*$; in the unstable band the fit reflects bounded wandering, not contraction. (b) Time for the beat-sampled error to settle below $5\%$ of the perturbation; the last stable gain ($K T^* = 2.1$) settles off scale at $16$, and unstable gains never settle. (c) Accumulated position offset, against the discrete prediction $|\vec {u}^*(0)| \left ( 1/K - T^*/2 \right )$; unstable gains wander off scale. (d) Fraction of transient beats on which the trim saturates: zero across the stable range, nonzero only past the stability boundary, where the saturation bounds the divergence}{figure.caption.24}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Prescribed trajectory under the velocity law ($K = 2.7$): the body follows the commanded velocity with a first-order lag, trailing the reference point by $|\vec  {u}^*_r| / K \approx 0.19$ body lengths through the cruise and cutting slightly inside the sharp corners, with the tracking error peaking at $0.28$. Position drift is not corrected, but the achieved path stays close to the course throughout---including the loop and the descent---and the body settles next to the course endpoint (final offset $0.06$).}}{30}{figure.caption.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:traj}{{21}{30}{Prescribed trajectory under the velocity law ($K = 2.7$): the body follows the commanded velocity with a first-order lag, trailing the reference point by $|\vec {u}^*_r| / K \approx 0.19$ body lengths through the cruise and cutting slightly inside the sharp corners, with the tracking error peaking at $0.28$. Position drift is not corrected, but the achieved path stays close to the course throughout---including the loop and the descent---and the body settles next to the course endpoint (final offset $0.06$)}{figure.caption.25}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.4}Prey Pursuit}{30}{subsection.4.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces Freehand trajectory under the velocity law ($K = 2.7$, cruise $0.4$): a hand-drawn closed course tracing a dragonfly. The body stays within $0.27$ body lengths of the reference point throughout and closes the course with a final offset of $0.09$.}}{31}{figure.caption.26}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:traj-drawn}{{22}{31}{Freehand trajectory under the velocity law ($K = 2.7$, cruise $0.4$): a hand-drawn closed course tracing a dragonfly. The body stays within $0.27$ body lengths of the reference point throughout and closes the course with a final offset of $0.09$}{figure.caption.26}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces Prey pursuit. (a) The line-of-sight command produces the classical pure-pursuit tail chase, with capture at $t^* \approx 7.5$ (star). (b) The body accelerates to the commanded closing speed ($J \approx 0.2$). $\gamma $ leans from $\gamma ^\text  {hover}$ toward alignment with the flight direction, $\psi _1$ follows its slaved schedule (\autoref {eq:psi1-slave}), and $(s_0^*, \psi _0)$ trim the force demand.}}{32}{figure.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pursuit}{{23}{32}{Prey pursuit. (a) The line-of-sight command produces the classical pure-pursuit tail chase, with capture at $t^* \approx 7.5$ (star). (b) The body accelerates to the commanded closing speed ($J \approx 0.2$). $\gamma $ leans from $\gamma ^\text {hover}$ toward alignment with the flight direction, $\psi _1$ follows its slaved schedule (\autoref {eq:psi1-slave}), and $(s_0^*, \psi _0)$ trim the force demand}{figure.caption.27}{}}
